00:01:18.956 --> 00:01:20.279
Here is a quick table:

24
00:01:20.559 --> 00:01:21.625
Code, and Why.

25
00:01:21.905 --> 00:01:24.465
MapInteger, LinkedHashSetK buckets:

26
00:01:24.745 --> 00:01:43.604
A LinkedHashSet gives O(1) add, remove and oldest (iterator().next()), which is the LRU tie-break inside a frequency. if (bucket.isEmpty()) buckets.remove(f); if (minFreq == f) minFreq = f + 1;: minFreq can only rise by one on a touch.

27
00:01:43.884 --> 00:01:47.895
No scan is needed. minFreq = 1 (in put of a new key):

28
00:01:48.175 --> 00:01:56.772
New keys have frequency 1, which is always the minimum. for 100 keys: hot.put(scan- + i, i), then hot key still cached:

29
00:01:57.052 --> 00:02:03.260
The scan keys all have frequency 1 and evict each other, while the hot key (frequency 6) survives.

30
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.417
An LRU cache would have lost it.

31
