00:06:49.654 --> 00:06:50.507
Part 8.

100
00:06:50.787 --> 00:06:51.918
Common mistakes.

101
00:06:53.168 --> 00:06:54.747
Using a heap for eviction.

102
00:06:55.027 --> 00:06:58.419
That's O(log n) and needs a decrease-key operation.

103
00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:02.197
Forgetting to update minFreq when the minimum bucket empties.

104
00:07:02.477 --> 00:07:06.488
Pure LFU without ageing: yesterday's hot item stays forever.

105
00:07:06.768 --> 00:07:11.163
Production policies (W-TinyLFU in Caffeine) decay counts.

106
00:07:12.513 --> 00:07:13.409
Part 9.

107
00:07:13.689 --> 00:07:15.033
Follow-up questions.

108
00:07:16.283 --> 00:07:17.157
Question.

109
00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:21.192
LRU versus LFU versus W-TinyLFU?

110
00:07:21.472 --> 00:07:22.795
Thirty-second answer:

111
00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:27.107
LRU is simple and adapts to recency, but it's flushed by scans.

112
00:07:27.387 --> 00:07:31.077
LFU resists scans but adapts slowly and needs ageing.

113
00:07:31.357 --> 00:07:43.283
W-TinyLFU (Caffeine) admits new items only if their approximate frequency (from a Count-Min sketch) beats the eviction victim's, plus a small LRU window for bursts.

114
00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:45.952
It's close to optimal on real workloads.

115
00:07:47.302 --> 00:07:48.177
Question.

116
00:07:48.457 --> 00:07:50.761
What eviction policies does Redis offer?

117
00:07:51.041 --> 00:08:02.966
Thirty-second answer: allkeys-lru, allkeys-lfu (approximate: an 8-bit log counter with decay), the volatile- variants that only evict keys with a TTL, and noeviction.

118
00:08:03.246 --> 00:08:07.513
Redis samples keys instead of keeping exact structures, to save memory.

